      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-223731",
      ":question/problem": "Prove the following theorem:\n\n> Let $a$, $b$, and $n$ be integers. If $gcd(a, n) = k > 1$ and $k\u2224b$, then the linear congruence $ax\u2261b(modn)$ has no solution.\n\nWe assume $gcd(a, n) = k > 1$, meaning that there exist $a_{1}, n_{1} \u2208 Z$ such that $a = a_{1} \\cdot$ {{field-1}} and $n = n_{1} \\cdot$ {{field-2}}. Also, $k$ {{field-3}} $b$."
