      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-224686",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following statement:\n\n> Let $a, b$, and $n$ be integers. If $gcd(a, n) = k > 1$ and $k\u2224b$, then the linear congruence $ax\u2261b(modn)$ has no solution.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $L1$: We are given that $gcd(a, n) = k > 1$ and $k\u2224b$.\n> $L2$: So, there exists $a_{1}, n_{1} \u2208 Z$ such that $a = a_{1}k$ and $n = n_{1}k$.\n> $L3$: Assume for a contradiction that $ax\u2261b(modn)$ has a solution.\n> $L4$: Then, there exists $x_{1} \u2208 Z$ such that $ax_{1}\u2261b(modn)$.\n> $L5$: So, there exists $c \u2208 Z$ such that $ax_{1} - b = nc$.\n> $L6$: Hence, $ax_{1} - nc = b$.\n> $L7$: As a result, $k(a_{1}x_{1} - n_{1}c) = b$.\n> $L8$: This implies that $k\u2223b$, which is a contradiction.\n> $L9$: Therefore, the assumption that $ax\u2261b(modn)$ has a solution is false.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ follows from line $L1$ by the\n{{field-1}}\nof\n{{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L5$ is equivalent to line $L4$ by the {{field-3}}\nof\n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe given proof is a proof by {{field-5}}."
